# [DD-13b.W23.3] `include` PARSES NOW, AND THIS FILE REPLACES THE ONE THAT
# ASSERTED IT DID NOT.
#
# `wave2_keyword.rxtin` was this fixture's predecessor: it carried an
# `include` line and asserted the NOT-IN-THIS-BUILD refusal, which was the
# K14 shape's witness — a real keyword, spelled correctly, in the format's
# own documentation, refused by name rather than as a typo. W23.3 builds
# the production, so the fixture's PREMISE shipped and no edit could keep
# it honest: a file named `wave2_keyword` asserting acceptance would be a
# lie about itself, and inverting an assertion in place is how a fixture
# stops meaning what its header says.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT OF THE WAVE TIER, stated so the absence is a decision: at
# this pin NO schema row sits strictly between this build's wave and the
# RESERVED sentinel, so `refuse_wave`'s NOT-IN-THIS-BUILD branch has no
# reachable input and no fixture can construct one (the table is
# compile-time). format_design §1.3 and w23_impl §2.3 both say so in
# advance — "its population is empty at the FINAL pin" — and the branch is
# kept because the NEXT wave's rollout is when it matters. W23-S3 arm 4
# is where that zero is reported honestly rather than silently;
# `version_reserved.rxtin` is the RESERVED tier's live witness, and it is
# the only one of the two tiers a build can reach today.
#
# LEG A ONLY: `include` is a HEAD declaration and the head has one parser
# (the seam ruling, w1_impl §1.1). Legs B and C never read these bytes.
# Resolving the path, subtracting the fragment from the entry set and
# splicing its blocks is W23.3a's — this file asserts the GRAMMAR and
# nothing about what the path names.
#
# [FINDINGS] B0 (lane findb0, 2026-09-26): the target was `common.rxt`, a
# LIBRARY carrying a file-level `description` — a fragment §2.5 forbids and
# that leg A now refuses once it reads fragments. It was the tree's one
# non-conforming include target; it now names a blocks-only fragment.
include "include_basic_frag.rxtfrag"

pattern a+
m "aaa" 0 3
